s09  The baseline: an LLM as a function
      f: V* -> Delta(V), and the three consequences that follow
==============================================================================

2.1  The object under study
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  vocabulary size |V|                24
  true source order                  2
  support per context                5 of 24 tokens
  train / dev / test tokens          48000 / 6000 / 6000

  sample:  agent call no the token reads one stops emits . token no model step a state with one with call no the
  # a known order-2 source, so the true distribution is available for comparison; a real LLM never offers this

2.2  Fitting the interpolated trigram
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  p_hat(w|u,v) = l3*c(u,v,w)/c(u,v) + l2*c(v,w)/c(v) + l1*c(w)/N
       interpolation keeps the support full; an unsmoothed trigram assigns zero to unseen triples and held-out cross-entropy diverges
  l1 (uni)  l2 (bi)  l3 (tri)  dev bits/token  dev PP
  --------  -------  --------  --------------  ------
      0.02      0.1      0.88          1.7674   3.404
      0.05      0.1      0.85          1.8041   3.492
       0.1      0.1       0.8          1.8697   3.655
      0.02      0.2      0.78          1.8904   3.707
       ...      ...       ...             ...     ...
       0.2      0.4       0.4          2.5505   5.858
  selected lambdas                   (0.02, 0.1, 0.88)
  # selected on dev, never on train: fitted on the training counts the search drives l3 -> 1, because the unsmoothed model is by construction the best explanation of what it memorised
  train cross-entropy                1.7206 bits/token
  test  cross-entropy                1.7849 bits/token
  test perplexity                    3.446 effective branches

2.3  Where the loss actually goes
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  H(p*, q) = H(p*) + D_KL(p* || q)
       cross-entropy splits into the entropy of the process, which no model can remove, and the divergence of the model from it, which is the only part training can touch
  H(p*)   irreducible entropy        1.6119 bits/token
  D_KL(p* || p_hat)  model gap       0.1712 bits/token
  H(p*) + D_KL                       1.7832 bits/token
  H(p*, p_hat)  computed directly    1.7832 bits/token
  [PASS] cross-entropy equals entropy plus divergence, to machine precision
         residual 1.643e-14 bits/token
  [PASS] the irreducible term dominates the model's total loss
         H(p*)=1.612 vs D_KL=0.171 bits/token
  # a perplexity number alone cannot tell you whether a model is weak or the process is noisy; only the split can, and the split needs a ground truth that production systems do not have

2.4  The divergence shrinks with data, the entropy does not
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  train tokens  D_KL(p*||p_hat)  test bits/token  test PP
  ------------  ---------------  ---------------  -------
           750           3.2468           4.8359   28.559
          3000           1.0804           2.7655      6.8
         12000           0.3052           1.9305    3.812
         48000           0.1707           1.7886    3.455
  [PASS] divergence from the true process decreases monotonically with data
         3.2468 > 1.0804 > 0.3052 > 0.1707

2.5  One forward pass, in full
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  context = ('the', 'model')
  token    p_hat   p_true  logit = log p_hat  -log2 p (bits)
  -----  -------  -------  -----------------  --------------
  and     0.4944   0.3344            -0.7044           1.016
  model  0.30232  0.39665            -1.1963           1.726
  grows  0.10421  0.25523            -2.2613           3.262
  step   0.01586        0            -4.1436           5.978
  stops  0.01346        0            -4.3083           6.216
  agent   0.0085  0.00528            -4.7682           6.879
  sum of all 24 probabilities        1
  H(p_hat) at this context           2.0846 bits
  [PASS] the output is a normalised distribution over the whole vocabulary
         |sum - 1| = 2.22e-16, min p = 7.823e-04
  # this vector is the model's entire output; a token is what a separate decoding rule does to it

2.6  The function is pure, therefore the model is stateless
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] identical context gives bit-identical output
         np.array_equal on float64, not allclose
  [PASS] intervening calls on other contexts leave the output unchanged
         52 calls between the two evaluations, max abs diff 0.0e+00
  [PASS] different contexts give different outputs
         D_KL = 5.701 bits

  # this is the single most consequential fact in the repo. The model has no memory, no session and no accumulating state. A conversation is a string that a program rebuilds and resubmits on every turn. Memory, tools, planning and orchestration are therefore not model features; they are things written around a stateless function, and S13 onwards is about what it takes to write them.

2.7  What 'context' means mechanically
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Truncate the conditioning set to the last n tokens and measure how far
  the prediction moves from the full-context prediction.
  tokens kept  mean D_KL vs full (bits)  max D_KL
  -----------  ------------------------  --------
            0                    2.2906    3.5725
            1                    2.1581    5.2218
            2                         0         0
  [PASS] truncating below the true order of the process destroys information
         keep 0: 2.291, keep 1: 2.158, keep 2: 0.0e+00 bits
  # the context window is a hard horizon, not a soft preference. Beyond the dependency length of the process it costs nothing to drop tokens; below it, information is gone and no decoding rule recovers it. Real text has dependencies thousands of tokens long, which is the entire argument for long windows -- and the reason S11's KV cache matters, since that horizon has to be paid for in memory.

  # where we are: the function is fixed, exact, stateless, and outputs a distribution rather than a token. Nothing above chose a token. Every system built on a model has to, and S10 is about the fact that the rule for doing it is a search with no tractable optimum

